efully:\n\nSource code provided:\n- pkg/format/color.go + color_test.go ✓\n- pkg/format/column.go + column_test.go ✓\n- pkg/format/size.go (NO test) ✗\n- pkg/sys/fileinfo.go + fileinfo_darwin.go + fileinfo_linux.go (NO test) ✗\n- pkg/testutils/difftest.go + difftest_test.go ✓\n\nMissing tests:\n1. pkg/format/size_test.go - tests for HumanSize (prd003 R3.1-R3.4)\n2. pkg/sys/ tests - tests for Stat, Lstat, FileMetadata (prd002 R2.1-R2.3)\n\nBoth are in release 00.0. The size_test.go is more straightforward and self-contained, whereas the sys tests would require filesystem fixtures and platform-specific handling. I'll prioritize the size_test.go task.\n\nLooking at the size.go implementation, I need to understand what HumanSize does: it takes a byte count and options, then formats it using either binary mode (base 1024 with suffixes like K, M, G, T, P, E) or SI mode (base 1000 with suffixes like kB, MB, GB, TB).\n\nThe formatting rules are: zero values return \"0\", values under 10 at the current unit level get one decimal place, values 10 and above get no decimals, and when no suffix is needed the result is an integer.\n\nNow I'm drafting the task specification as YAML, referencing the product requirements document, the implementation file being tested, and using the existing color_test.go as a style reference for how tests should be structured. for this package)\n      - pkg/format/column_test.go (test style reference for this package)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/size_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for HumanSize covering prd003 R3.1-R3.4\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test HumanSize with Binary mode (base-1024) across unit boundaries: bytes, K, M, G, T, P, E suffixes per prd003 R3.1, R3.2\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test HumanSize with SI mode (base-1000) across unit boundaries: bytes, kB, MB, GB, TB suffixes per prd003 R3.1, R3.2\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test decimal formatting: values below 10 at the chosen unit produce one decimal place, values 10 or above produce no decimal place per prd003 R3.3\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test zero-byte input returns \\\"0\\\" regardless of Binary/SI mode per prd003 R3.4\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Test boundary values at exact unit thresholds (1024, 1048576 for binary; 1000, 1000000 for SI) to verify correct unit selection\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Test negative values are handled correctly (HumanSize accepts int64, negative values should format with the negative sign)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the table-driven test pattern established in color_test.go and column_test.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Package is format_test (external test package) matching existing test files\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Copyright header and SPDX license identifier matching existing test files in pkg/format/\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"pkg/format/size_test.go exists and compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/format/ passes with all new tests green\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Binary mode tests cover at least bytes, K, M, G, T, P, E suffixes\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"SI mode tests cover at least bytes, kB, MB, GB, TB suffixes\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Decimal formatting tests verify both sub-10 and 10+ value formatting\"\n\nI'm setting up the test file structure with the external test package format, matching the copyright and license headers from the existing test files in the package, then defining acceptance criteria to ensure the test file compiles and covers all the required unit suffixes and decimal formatting rules.\n\nNow I'm checking the constraint requirements—I've got 4 PRD sub-requirements covered, 6 task requirements, 6 acceptance criteria, and 3 design decisions, which all fit within the specified ranges. Looking at the line budget by examining similar test files in the codebase to estimate the scope. Looking at the code's use of `math.Abs(size)`, negative values seem to be handled, but that's not explicitly stated in the PRD—just R3.1 through R3.4. Since I need to anchor task requirements directly to PRD requirements and avoid inventing design decisions, I should replace R6 with something that actually maps to the documented spec. With four PRD sub-requirements, I can structure my task requirements to stay within the 5-8 range while keeping each one tied back to the source material. I'm refining R6 to focus on boundary conditions—specifically testing values that equal exactly one unit to verify the formatting logic handles cases like 1024 correctly. Then I need to confirm the file path structure for the PRD document, which should follow the naming convention `docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml`.\n\nLet me verify my task stays within constraints: I have exactly four sub-requirements (R3.1 through R3.4), which meets the maximum of four, and since this is the only task, the dependency should be -1. I should also check that I'm not duplicating any existing test coverage from the source code.\n\nLooking at what's already there, I see test files for colors and columns, but nothing for sizing yet, so this is genuinely new work. The constraints are clear: exactly one task, no tool calls, and output the YAML directly. I'm also thinking about whether to emphasize matching the existing test patterns in the required reading and design decisions so the implementation stays consistent with the codebase style.\n\nFor the scope, 150-250 lines is reasonable for a test file, so I should make sure my task description sets appropriate expectations. I'm ready to produce the final output now. Looking at the release 00.0 tasks, I need to weigh size_test.go against the sys tests. Since both are test tasks for existing code, I'll apply the canonical ordering rules: pkg/format comes before pkg/sys alphabetically, and the test suite spec should be referenced at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-00. This confirms size_test.go is the right priority. I'm settling on format tests first since they're part of the earliest incomplete release, and the foundation needs to be solid before building on it. Let me verify the naming convention one more time—source files like size.go should have corresponding size_test.go test files—then I'll write up the response.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38004,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38004},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00e1e0cf-c01b-448e-bf07-4cd83caa1b41","uuid":"e3493356-eef3-4c5d-8617-88f9777cd76b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TSYnug9QX1QJhjrx3AMNrC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications accessible to implementers. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations using AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds per-utility Go implementations, `pkg/testutils/` provides the differential testing harness, `pkg/sys/` wraps platform-divergent syscalls, `pkg/format/` provides shared formatting, and `magefiles/` exposes build targets. Specs live in `docs/`.\n\n**Current implementation:** Source code exists for `pkg/format/` (color.go, column.go, size.go + tests for color and column), `pkg/sys/` (fileinfo.go + platform files, no tests), and `pkg/testutils/` (difftest.go + tests). No `cmd/` utilities exist on the working branch. Notably, `pkg/format/size_test.go` is absent despite the implementation being complete.\n\n**Current release:** Release 00.0 (foundational infrastructure) is the earliest incomplete release. `pkg/format`, `pkg/sys`, and `pkg/testutils` have implementations but are missing test coverage — specifically `size_test.go` for format and all tests for sys.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nRelease 00.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Within it, `pkg/format/size.go` has a complete implementation covering prd003 R3.1–R3.4 but no test file, while `color_test.go` and `column_test.go` already exist. Following canonical ordering (P8: alphabetical tie-breaking on file path), `pkg/format/` comes before `pkg/sys/`. The single highest-priority task is the missing `size_test.go`.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Tests for pkg/format HumanSize -- prd003 R3.1-R3.4\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml (R3.1-R3.4 human-readable size conversion requirements)\n      - pkg/format/size.go (HumanSize and HumanSizeOpts implementation under test)\n      - pkg/format/color_test.go (test style and package conventions reference)\n      - pkg/format/column_test.go (table-driven test pattern reference)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-00.0.yaml (test suite specification for release 00.0)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/size_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for HumanSize and HumanSizeOpts covering prd003 R3.1-R3.4\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test HumanSize with Binary mode (HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true}) across unit boundaries covering bytes through E suffix per prd003 R3.1, R3.2\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test HumanSize with SI mode (HumanSizeOpts{Binary: false}) across unit boundaries covering bytes through TB suffix per prd003 R3.1, R3.2\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test decimal formatting: values below 10 at the chosen unit produce one decimal place (e.g., \\\"1.5K\\\"), values 10 or above produce no decimal place (e.g., \\\"15K\\\") per prd003 R3.3\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test zero-byte input returns \\\"0\\\" for both Binary and SI modes per prd003 R3.4\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Test boundary values at exact unit thresholds (1024 and multiples for binary, 1000 and multiples for SI) to verify correct suffix selection per prd003 R3.1\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Test that values below the base (< 1024 for binary, < 1000 for SI) return integer format with no suffix per prd003 R3.2\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use table-driven test pattern with named sub-tests matching the style in color_test.go and column_test.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use external test package format_test matching existing test files in pkg/format/\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Include copyright header and SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT matching existing test files\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"pkg/format/size_test.go exists and compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/format/ passes with all new tests green\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Binary mode tests verify output for at least bytes, K, M, G, T, P, and E suffixes\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"SI mode tests verify output for at least bytes, kB, MB, GB, and TB suffixes\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Decimal formatting tests include at least one value below 10 and one value at or above 10 for both unit modes\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"Zero input produces \\\"0\\\" in both Binary and SI modes\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38004,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38004},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00e1e0cf-c01b-448e-bf07-4cd83caa1b41","uuid":"28540b1f-b2d7-41df-b220-244db302f3c7"}
